In article <1993Dec1.154927.29435@ee.surrey.ac.uk>, ph21pb@ph.surrey.ac.uk (Paul
Blay) says:
>
>I have a probably unworkable idea to make nethack for PC
>rather faster to play.  One of the biggest waits is when
>starting/saving/quiting.   [snip snip]

I somehow don't see this as much of a problem.  Usually
I don't play the game unless I have a couple hours to
devote to it.  Waiting 3-5 minutes for start/stop is
relatively minor.  If nothing else, if does discourage
saved-game abuses.

>Monsters using items, I find it really annoying when the
>monster uses one shot items (scrolls/potions) as soon as
>they see you. I would like there to be at least some
>chance of getting the potion of gain level from the
>monster especially as if it only takes 1/2 attacks to
>kill it.

Annoying as it is, I've usually been satisfied with the
current implementation.  They do take time to see something
they like, walk to it, pick it up, and then use it.  Often
that gives you enough time to hurt them.  This feature
greatly encourages the use of Stealth and Ranged attacks.
Making the monsters hesitate more really devalues those tactics.

>This goes for some other items as well, How do monsters
>'know' that a amulet of lifesaving is a good thing to
>wear, couldnt we have some chance of monsters not
>wearing stuff when they probably have no idea what it
>does.

Here, I'd agree.  They seem to know whether to aim a wand
at you or themselves, whether to throw a potion, etc.
There are times where I've wanted to leave a cursed magic
item around to see what happened when a monster experimented
with it.  Right now, the only thing they don't seem to
recognize is blessed/cursed status.

>Oh a final thing on the savegame/startgame times. When
>you get into the final levels, surely it is impossible
>to return to the main dungeon and all the rest of the
>save game data can be scrapped. Cutting down on disk
>space used and the time taken to load.

If it is indeed true, then this does look attractive.
I'm just left wondering how much more play time you have
remaining anyway.  If you were to begin a particular
play session just before entering the Planes, how long
should it take to wade through the five levels?  I am assuming
that you would try to take each portal as soon as you saw it.

>Erm finally it seems unfair that the random +2 to -2
>level change for new man/new woman polymorphing can only
>result in -ve change when you are already level 30.
>Ditto for stat changes when you were 18/** 18 for the
>lot.

I guess all that can be said here is: Don't polymorph.
At level 30, you should be more powerful than anything
else anyway.  There are a couple things you can wear to
resist the traps.  You probably don't want to eat any chameleons.

Good luck!
